WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.790
- Americans pay a lot for their medicine,

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.860
and it only seems to be getting worse.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.820
US spending on prescription
drugs has increased by 76%

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.260
in the last 20 years.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.630
Today we pay around twice as much

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.340
as other wealthy nations on average.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.080
- This can have devastating effects

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.060
on the individuals who rely on them,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.220
with one in three Americans reporting

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.530
that they skipped prescribed medications

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.270
because of the cost.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.190
Many others have gone into debt

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.490
or even suffered bankruptcy
over high drug prices.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.170
- But there is another option

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.230
that Americans are increasingly
taking advantage of,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.080
buying prescription drugs
from other countries.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.930
The practice is not without
health and legal risks,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.600
but the price differences can be so huge

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.430
it's understandable
that people are willing

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.570
to take a gamble.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.580
For many it might be the only way

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.950
to access life-saving medications.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.110
- But before you pop
over the Mexican border

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.010
for some insulin,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.400
or log onto a Canadian
website for asthma inhalers,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.980
you should know how much
you can really save,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.990
whether the risks are worth the reward,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.856
and how we got into this
mess in the first place.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.780
- Why are drugs so expensive in the US?

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.204
The short answer is that
here pharmaceutical companies

00:01:18.204 --> 00:01:20.910
are allowed to charge whatever they want.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.490
Virtually all other wealthy
nations have regulatory bodies

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.270
that determine how much
new drugs can cost,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.220
based on how medically effective they are.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.530
If the drug company
doesn't like the price cap,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.570
the drug won't get approved.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.148
- By contrast, the FDA
must approve every drug

00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:38.790
as long as it's considered
safe and effective,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.530
and has no say in the price.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.470
Individual insurers and
hospitals can negotiate

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.830
with drug companies, but because
the market is so fractured,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.450
they don't have much leverage.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.310
The only organization big enough

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.410
to throw its weight around, Medicare,

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.930
is legally prohibited from haggling.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.400
They're required by law

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.610
to cover nearly every
drug the FDA approves

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.730
at whatever price the
drug companies ask for,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.130
the upshot is that Americans have access

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.950
to a much wider range of prescriptions

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.420
but at prices so high

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.030
they're effectively inaccessible to many.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.647
- It's not surprising that
many Americans have decided,

00:02:14.647 --> 00:02:17.700
"Hey, if my government won't
do anything about drug prices,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.980
I'll just buy from a country that will,"

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.840
this can mean dropping into a pharmacy

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.250
while on vacation abroad,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.590
or ordering from an international website.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.150
It's surprisingly easy,

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.490
and there are a lot of
savings to be found,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.590
especially on name-brand drugs.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.240
So why not go for it?

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.390
Well, for one thing, it's illegal.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.430
- US law prohibits the importation

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.860
of prescription medication
from other countries,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.630
even those that are FDA-approved

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.790
and manufactured in America.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.040
But in reality you're very unlikely

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.480
to get into trouble for it.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.960
The FDA specifically allows
agents to overlook infractions,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.380
as long as the quantity is
obviously for personal use,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.790
say, three months worth,

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.030
and the drug doesn't pose
any serious health risks.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.220
- Although the possibility
of legal jeopardy

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.110
might not be much of a deterrent,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.780
there are still health
and safety risks involved.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.613
- To talk about them,

00:03:07.613 --> 00:03:10.028
we've invited some real
healthcare professionals

00:03:10.028 --> 00:03:11.280
on the show.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.000
Dr. Alok Patel and Sheena
Williams from PBS Vitals.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.270
- It's true, brand-name
prescription prices in the US

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.995
far exceed those of many other countries.

00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:22.110
And as we've been hearing
from Philip and Julia,

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.050
in most cases, importing
prescription drugs into the US

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.050
for personal use is
prohibited and illegal.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.330
Some might say that's to
protect pharma's bottom line

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.700
but the safety issues are very real.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.080
- Here's an interesting fact,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.120
according to the World
Health Organization,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.890
one in 10 medical products found

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.720
in low and middle-income countries

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.910
are either substandard or counterfeit.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.740
In a separate study, scientists found

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.590
that up to 41% of tested
drugs purchased abroad

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.870
failed quality specification,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.780
that says something about the
oversight from the US FDA,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.610
which is really considered
the gold standard

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:54.849
around the world.

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:56.880
(upbeat music)
(water slurping)

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.590
- But for some the financial
benefits outweigh the risks.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.780
If you're in the market
to buy prescription drugs

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.570
outside the US, here are
some helpful tips for you

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.160
to be able to spot a counterfeit.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.040
Number one, get a prescription
from a US physician

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.460
and make sure you're familiar
with the medication's size,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.190
shape, color, and taste.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.980
If there are cracks,
discoloration, or stickiness,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.070
throw those away, as they're
probably counterfeit.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.350
Check out these counterfeits,

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.860
notice how the label and print
have different font styles

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.360
and printing patterns.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.040
Also, in some cases

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.160
the drugs might be smaller
or larger in shape.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.930
Pills should be in
their original packaging

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.390
and not loose or in a
plastic bag or envelope,

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.770
that'd be just weird.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:42.960
The most common counterfeit
prescription drugs

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.970
include erectile dysfunction drugs

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.880
such as Viagra, Levitra, and Cialis.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.340
Cholesterol medication like Lipitor,

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.310
diet pills, antihistamines, antivirals

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.620
like those used to treat HIV and AIDS,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.070
and expensive chemotherapeutic drugs.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.270
- You also want to consider

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.890
that not all countries are the same

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.840
when it comes to making your purchase.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.660
Oversight in some places is
a lot better than others,

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.000
your odds are a lot better
if your meds are coming

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.080
from well-regulated countries
like Canada, New Zealand,

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.080
Australia, Turkey, or
most of Western Europe.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.700
Another thing to look out for,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.050
double-check that the pharmacy

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.760
you are purchasing your medicine from

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.100
is licensed in the country of origin.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.270
There are several resources

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.880
to find safer and
affordable medicines abroad.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.510
The Canadian International
Pharmacy Association runs a site

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.260
that can help you find
legitimate certified pharmacies.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.140
Another option is pharmacychecker.com.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.250
They compare prices

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.530
and the legitimacy of prescription drugs,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.360
available online and abroad.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.000
Finally, check out the
Medicines Quality Database

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.700
by the US Pharmacopeial Convention.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.730
They've compiled alerts issued
for poor-quality medicines

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:54.480
found in Africa, Asia,
Eastern Europe, Latin America,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.500
and the Caribbean.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.140
- One more thing, if you've
been prescribed something

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.390
that is outside your price range,

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.970
ask your doctor for a
generic prescription.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.020
A recent study found

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.470
that 88% of the most commonly
prescribed generic drugs

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.153
in the United States
were almost 68% cheaper

00:06:10.153 --> 00:06:13.500
than purchasing a generic in
Canada, what's up with that?

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.260
- If you want more information
about medical tourism,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.670
come on over to Vitals

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.270
- Back to you, dynamic duo at Two Cents

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.040
- Beyond your own personal health,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.810
both medical and financial,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.880
there's another reason
that some discourage

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.010
the practice of buying cheap drugs abroad,

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.320
it's bad for the industry.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.880
Pharmaceutical companies claim

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.456
that they're high prices are reflective

00:06:34.456 --> 00:06:38.340
of the steep cost of research
and development of new drugs.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.315
If everyone paid low prices,
they'd have to cut back

00:06:41.315 --> 00:06:44.070
on developing new drugs
for serious ailments

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.087
like cancer and Alzheimer's.

00:06:46.087 --> 00:06:47.970
"I know you want a good bargain

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.550
but you're not on the side of cancer,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.633
are you?"
- Boo!

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.410
- This is the same argument they use

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.200
against any kind of price
controls or regulation,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.620
that it will stifle the future
of pharmaceutical research,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.440
beside the fact that they're
basically asking Americans

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.230
to subsidize low prices
for the rest of the world,

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:11.250
there are some reasons to be
dubious about this explanation.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.190
- For one thing, prices have
been rising steadily on drugs

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.170
that haven't changed in decades,

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:19.440
and thus require no
research and development,

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.500
that would be like if Apple
never updated their iPhone

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:26.160
since 2008, but raised the
price on it every year,

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.950
you'd only buy that if
your life depended on it.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:32.550
- Also, despite claims of high
costs, recent analysis found

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.242
that drug companies actually have plenty

00:07:34.242 --> 00:07:35.850
of financial wiggle room.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.440
They make more than enough money

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.300
to cover the costs of R&amp;D,

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:42.510
and have enough leftover
for large profit margins.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:43.590
Many other companies

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.950
like auto manufacturers spend more on R&amp;D

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.960
with smaller profit margins.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.960
- In fact, of the top 10
pharmaceutical companies,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.740
nine spend more on
marketing than research.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.780
That's right, those incessant ads

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.280
that tell you to ask your doctor

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.140
if such and such is right for you,

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.810
are more responsible for high drug prices

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.820
than the cost of innovation,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.730
which means we could
have fewer of those ads

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.590
and lower drug prices?

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.330
Sounds like a win-win to me.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.880
- Growing more frustrated with high prices

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.340
and more skeptical of
the industry's excuses,

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.190
a majority of Americans now
support the legalization

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.440
of importing drugs from other countries.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:26.070
In 2021, the Biden administration
signed an executive order

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.010
allowing states to legally
purchase inexpensive drugs

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.750
from Canada, which could be good news

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.470
for millions of patients
burdened by high drug prices.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.570
- And yet there's something strange

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.400
about the whole situation.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.610
Rather than just regulating
the price of drugs here,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.550
we're going to let people
import America- made drugs

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.610
from other countries that are
willing to regulate prices?

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.724
- It does seem ridiculous,
especially considering

00:08:49.724 --> 00:08:53.220
that the vast majority
of Americans also support

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.770
some kind of regulation on drug prices

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.240
but the pharmaceutical industry

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.790
still has a lot of sway in Washington,

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:03.450
and it's much easier to kill
a bill than to pass one.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.520
- Meanwhile, millions
of Americans are forced

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:06.930
to weigh their health needs

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.620
against practices that may be
medically or legally risky.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.080
It may not be a viable long-term solution,

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.357
but in the current American
healthcare landscape,

00:09:15.357 --> 00:09:19.440
you can't blame people for
doing what they need to survive.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.163
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.210
- If you want more info and advice

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.460
on navigating the crazy, confusing,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.980
but mortally important
world of healthcare,

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.735
head over to PBS Vitals.

